 # Pixel Intensity Evaluation with Scene Aggregates
 
-Also called: AIV/ABV Integration, Aggregated Boundary Visibility, Aggregated Visibility Functions Integration
+Also called: Aggregated Boundary Visibility (ABV), Aggregated Visibility Functions Integration, AIV/ABV Integration
 
 ## Overview
-Pixel Intensity Evaluation with Scene Aggregates is a method for rendering volumetric data that replaces order-dependent ray marching with a precomputed visibility aggregation model [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of correctly blending scene aggregate contributions with external background objects while handling inter-voxel correlation, which traditional volumetric models fail to distinguish [S1, unofficial]. Unlike sibling schemes such as [Intensity](52-intensity.md) or [Accumulate](52-accumulate.md) that process samples sequentially, this method evaluates pixel intensity by integrating radiance over the pixel footprint using Aggregated Visibility Functions (AVF), specifically the Aggregated Boundary Visibility (ABV) [S1, unofficial].
+Pixel Intensity Evaluation with Scene Aggregates is a method for rendering volumetric data by integrating radiance over a pixel footprint using aggregated visibility functions to handle inter-voxel correlation and background blending without order-dependent ray marching [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of correctly blending scene aggregate contributions with external background objects, which standard volumetric models fail to distinguish when voxels have identical local properties but different spatial correlations [S1, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [X-Ray Volume Rendering](11336-x-ray-volume-rendering.md) by explicitly modeling the semi-transparency of geometry primitives via boundary visibility, rather than relying solely on scalar accumulation or extremum selection [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method evaluates pixel intensity by integrating incoming radiance over the pixel footprint, utilizing precomputed visibility terms to handle occlusion and transparency without per-ray traversal [S1, unofficial].
+The method evaluates pixel intensity by integrating incoming radiance over the pixel footprint, utilizing precomputed Aggregated Boundary Visibility (ABV) and Aggregated Interior Visibility (AIV) terms to approximate the visibility of scene primitives [S1, unofficial].
 
